# Package update @VERSION@ (signed repo @REPO@): no reinstall

Internal test release for the CyberHygiene Project development team. Not an official Rocky Linux product (`NOTICE.txt`).

## What it fixes

- **`chp-identity-client` 0.2.3: no more false SELinux alarms from fapolicyd.** fapolicyd looks up local accounts
  only, but the host asks Kanidm first, and SELinux denies fapolicyd that socket. Each fapolicyd start logged about
  four denials. SELinux module `chp_kanidm` 1.1 now records those denials silently. The access is **still denied**;
  only the log noise is gone (requirements row 49, owner decision 2026-10-25).
- **`chp-site` 0.7.3:** `chp-site validate` no longer runs a template renderer found next to the site folder, which
  could be on any mounted volume. It uses only `/usr/share/chp-templates`, or a path you name.

Every other package is the same build as in update2.

## Apply it (each 0.2.0 host)

1. **Check this folder** as `0.2.0/VERIFY.md` describes: `SHA256SUMS.asc` against the project key, then
   `shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS`.
2. Copy `chp-repo-@REPO@.tar.gz` to the host, then unpack it over the local repo as root:

       rm -rf /var/lib/chp/repo/*
       tar -xzf chp-repo-@REPO@.tar.gz -C /var/lib/chp/repo --strip-components=1

3. Upgrade (a host without one of these packages just skips it):

       dnf --enablerepo=chp upgrade chp-identity-client chp-site

## Expect one AIDE alert, then accept it

The updated packages show in the next daily check. In the `aide-accept` summary they are listed under
**"Explained by signed package updates"**. Accept them as the ISSO or a named delegate:

    chp-site aide-accept --approver "Your Name" --reason "@VERSION@: chp-identity-client 0.2.3 + chp-site 0.7.3"
